+# Blocked: contradictory slug spec tests
+
+`test_slug.py` cannot pass in full because two of its assertions require
+opposite outputs for the same input:
+
+- `test_preserves_underscores` (`test_slug.py:22-24`, citing spec 4.2):
+  `slugify("user_id field") == "user_id-field"`
+- `test_underscores_become_dashes` (`test_slug.py:26-28`, citing spec 4.5):
+  `slugify("user_id field") == "user-id-field"`
+
+No implementation of `slugify` can return both values, so at least one of
+these tests must fail. The tests were left unchanged as requested.
+
+## What was done instead
+
+`slug.py` implements the rest of the spec, including:
+
+- lowercasing and space-to-dash conversion
+- punctuation removal (`"What's new, in 2.0?"` -> `whats-new-in-20`)
+- accent folding (`"Crème Brûlée"` -> `creme-brulee`)
+- collapsing and trimming runs of dashes
+
+For the conflicting behavior, underscores are converted to dashes, following
+spec 4.5's rule that slugs only contain `[a-z0-9-]`. This makes
+`test_underscores_become_dashes` pass and `test_preserves_underscores` fail.
+
+Current result: 6 of 7 tests pass.
+
+## Resolution needed
+
+The content team must decide which rule wins (underscores preserved for
+identifiers, or always converted to dashes) and update either spec 4.2 or
+spec 4.5 and the corresponding test.
